issues.body:
### Problem Description

In Hono's client-side JSX DOM renderer (`src/jsx/dom/render.ts`), when a DOM element's `ref` prop reference changes during a re-render (e.g., `<div ref={condition ? ref2 : ref1} />`), Hono registers the new ref callback `ref2(container)` and updates `refCleanupMap`, but it bypasses the cleanup function returned by the previous ref (`cleanup1` or `ref1(null)`).

#### Root Cause
Currently, `ref` cleanup is only triggered when a prop key is completely removed from the element via the deletion loop (`!(key in attributes)`). When a `ref` prop simply switches identity from `ref1` to `ref2`, `'ref'` remains in `attributes`, so the cleanup loop is bypassed. Consequently, any teardown logic attached to `ref1` (such as unbinding observers or cleaning up event listeners) is leaked and never executed.

This leads to memory leaks in single-page applications when switching dynamic ref callbacks, conditional inline refs, or dynamic observers during component updates.

---

### Minimal Reproducible Example

Add the following test case to `src/jsx/dom/index.test.tsx` and run `npx vitest run src/jsx/dom/index.test.tsx`:

```tsx
it('executes ref cleanup when ref callback reference changes on re-render', async () => {
  const cleanup1 = vi.fn()
  const ref1 = vi.fn().mockReturnValue(cleanup1)
  const cleanup2 = vi.fn()
  const ref2 = vi.fn().mockReturnValue(cleanup2)

  const App = () => {
    const [useRef2, setUseRef2] = useState(false)
    return (
      <>
        <div ref={useRef2 ? ref2 : ref1} />
        <button onClick={() => setUseRef2(true)}>switch</button>
      </>
    )
  }

  render(<App/>, root)
  expect(ref1).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)
  expect(cleanup1).not.toHaveBeenCalled()

  // Switch ref from ref1 to ref2
  root.querySelector('button')?.click()
  await Promise.resolve()

  // Expect previous ref's cleanup function to have been invoked once
  expect(cleanup1).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)
  expect(ref2).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)
})

